        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 966,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Puts values in order, and says where each came from. The positions are as useful as the values: sorting joint speeds gives the joints from slowest to fastest; sorting distances gives the neighbours from nearest to farthest.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.sort <dim>\nWithout a dim, along the last (each row of a matrix is sorted on its own). With one, a\ndim box appears.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim sorted along.\n\ndescending (checkbox, also an inlet):\nLargest first.\n\nstable (checkbox, also an inlet):\nEqual values keep their original order.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsorted:\nThe values in order.\n\nindices:\nFor each place in the order, the position the value came from. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n5 3 8 1 9 sorted is 1 3 5 8 9; the 1 was at position 3, the 3 at position 1, and so on.\n\nRELATED:\nt.argsort gives only the positions. t.topk gives just the largest (or smallest) few, quicker than sorting everything. np.sort is the numpy version. t.take_along_dim puts other data in the same order.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 950,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Puts values in order, and says where each came from. The positions are as useful as the values: sorting joint speeds gives the joints from slowest to fastest; sorting distances gives the neighbours from nearest to farthest.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.sort <dim>\nWithout a dim, along the last (each row of a matrix is sorted on its own). With one, a\ndim box appears.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet; only when a dim was given):\nThe dim sorted along.\n\ndescending (checkbox, also an inlet):\nLargest first.\n\nstable (checkbox, also an inlet):\nEqual values keep their original order.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsorted:\nThe values in order.\n\nindices:\nFor each place in the order, the position the value came from. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n5 3 8 1 9 sorted is 1 3 5 8 9; the 1 was at position 3, the 3 at position 1, and so on.\n\nRELATED:\nt.argsort gives only the positions. t.topk gives just the largest (or smallest) few, quicker than sorting everything. np.sort is the numpy version. t.take_along_dim puts other data in the same order."
        },
